  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://dbpedia.org/property/itWasTheEndOfTheGreatDepression;CanadaHadRecentlyEnteredWorldWarIi;AndVancouverStillBoreTheScarsOfTheTumultuous1930s.AfterTheStockMarketCrashOf1929,AttractedByVancouver’sTemperateClimate,HordesOfCanadiansHadFlockedToTheCityOnlyToFindLittleWorkAndEndUpSettlingInMakeshiftShantytownsAnd%22hoboJungles%22.TheEnactmentOfTheUnemploymentInsuranceActIn1940AndABuoyedEconomyHelpedTemperSomeOfTheDesperation,ButManyMenWereStillCaughtInTheCycleOfPovertyAndHomelessness,AndAlcoholismWasWidespread.BobStaceyHadWitnessedTheManySuccessesGodWasCreatingThroughTheRescueMissionInNycAndFeltCalledToHelpTheStrugglingMenAndVancouver.HeRaised$1,000InStartUpCostsByGettingTheWordOutOnReverendArnoldBennett’sDailyRadioProgram,MorningDevotions.HeRecruitedVolunteersWhoWantedToServeThePoor,HadSuppliesDonatedByLocalChurches,AndSecuredRentForTheUpstairsOf10PowellStreetAt$25AMonth.TheFacilityCouldSeat60People,HadASmallKitchenAndSixBedsForMenToStayTheNight.EveryEvening,Bob,AlongsideChurchGroups,Choirs,AndOtherVolunteers,WouldOfferAChapelServiceWithSoupAndASandwichServedToGuestsAfterward.UgmWasOpenEveryDay,AndBobWouldReachOutToThoseInNeedInTheNeighbourhoodAndGiveHelpToAllWhoAsked.DuringTheDay,AUgmVehicleWouldPickUpDonatedFoodFromWoodward’sFoodDepartmentDowntown,AsWellAsOtherLocalBakeries.OnDecember25th,1940,UgmHeldItsFirstEverChristmasDinnerForTheCommunityAndServedOver100People.Today,UgmServesOver3500MealsAtItsAnnualChristmasDinner,AndOver260,000MealsAnnually.We’veExpandedOurProgramsAndServicesSignificantlyAndGrownToFill9LocationsThroughoutMetroVancouverAndTheCityOfMission.It’sTakenVisionaryLeadershipAndStrongFaithInGod’sProvisionToBringUgmToWhereItIsToday.GodIsAlwaysAtTheHelmOfUgm,ButHereAreTheMenWhoseSuperiorLeadershipHasHelpedGuideTheOrganizationToWhereItIsTodayGordon(bob)R.Stacey1940–1943LeonardKlassen1943–1980MauriceMcelrea1980–2008WilliamB.Mollard2008–PresentCompanyName">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">It was the end of the Great Depression; Canada had recently entered World War II; and Vancouver still bore the scars of the tumultuous 1930s. After the stock market crash of 1929, attracted by Vancouver’s temperate climate, hordes of Canadians had flocked to the city only to find little work and end-up settling in makeshift shantytowns and "hobo jungles". The enactment of the Unemployment Insurance Act in 1940 and a buoyed economy helped temper some of the desperation, but many men were still caught in the cycle of poverty and homelessness, and alcoholism was widespread.

Bob Stacey had witnessed the many successes God was creating through the rescue mission in NYC and felt called to help the struggling men and Vancouver. He raised $1,000 in start-up costs by getting the word out on Reverend Arnold Bennett’s daily radio program, Morning Devotions. He recruited volunteers who wanted to serve the poor, had supplies donated by local churches, and secured rent for the upstairs of 10 Powell Street at $25 a month.

The facility could seat 60 people, had a small kitchen and six beds for men to stay the night. Every evening, Bob, alongside church groups, choirs, and other volunteers, would offer a chapel service with soup and a sandwich served to guests afterward.

UGM was open every day, and Bob would reach out to those in need in the neighbourhood and give help to all who asked. During the day, a UGM vehicle would pick up donated food from Woodward’s food department downtown, as well as other local bakeries. On December 25th, 1940, UGM held its first ever Christmas Dinner for the community and served over 100 people.

Today, UGM serves over 3500 meals at its annual Christmas dinner, and over 260,000 meals annually. We’ve expanded our programs and services significantly and grown to fill 9 locations throughout Metro Vancouver and the city of Mission. It’s taken visionary leadership and strong faith in God’s provision to bring UGM to where it is today. God is always at the helm of UGM, but here are the men whose superior leadership has helped guide the organization to where it is today:

Gordon (Bob) R. Stacey: 1940 – 1943
Leonard Klassen: 1943 – 1980
Maurice McElrea: 1980 – 2008
William B. Mollard: 2008 – present
  company name</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
